  "ai_overview": {
    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "ACE inhibitors and ARBs (Angiotensin Receptor Blockers) work equally well to lower blood pressure, but have a better safety and tolerability profile.",
        "snippet_links": [
          {
            "link": "https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/HYPERTENSIONAHA.120.16667"
          }
        ],
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "have a better safety and tolerability profile"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Effectiveness"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Blood pressure control: Both ACE inhibitors and ARBs reduce blood pressure equally by inhibiting the renin-angiotensin system."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Cardiovascular outcomes: Large studies show no significant difference between the two classes regarding heart attacks, strokes, heart failure, or cardiovascular death."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Safety and Side Effects"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Dry cough: ACE inhibitors frequently cause a persistent dry cough (due to a buildup of bradykinin), whereas ARBs do not."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Angioedema: Serious swelling of the face or throat is rare, but significantly more common with ACE inhibitors than ARBs."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Discontinuation: Patients are less likely to stop treatment on an ARB due to side effects."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Shared risks: Both classes carry a risk of high potassium levels (hyperkalemia) and low blood pressure (hypotension), and both are strictly contraindicated during pregnancy."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Clinical Recommendation"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Current guidelines from major cardiology groups list both ACE inhibitors and ARBs as top-tier, first-line options for hypertension."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "However, modern large-scale comparative data suggest that ARBs should be preferentially prescribed over ACE inhibitors as an initial choice because they provide identical blood pressure control with fewer adverse events."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Patients already doing well and tolerating an ACE inhibitor do not routinely need to switch."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you'd like, let me know:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Are you or a patient currently taking one of these medications ?"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Are you experiencing any side effects like a cough?"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "I can provide more tailored information or discuss next steps with your provider."
      }
